/-!
# The Koszul formula for the derived completion of a sheaf of modules, Tag 0A0E

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site, let `f_1, …, f_r` be global sections of `𝒪` and let `K ∈ D(𝒪)`.
[Stacks, Tag 0A0E] asserts that the derived completion of `K` along the ideal sheaf generated by
`f_1, …, f_r` is computed by the Koszul complexes:

`K^∧ = R lim (K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 K_n)`, `K_n = K(𝒪, f_1^n, …, f_r^n)`,

where — as the source's proof spells out — `K_n` is the Koszul *cochain* complex in degrees
`-r, …, 0`, that is the `𝒪`-dual `Hom_𝒪(K^n, 𝒪)` of the Koszul complex `K^n` in degrees
`0, …, r`. This file proves that formula.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedTensorHomInterchange_koszulPowerComplex`: the
  interchange morphism of [Stacks, Tag 0BYU] is invertible at a Koszul complex.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorRightUnitIso`: the right unit constraint
  `K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪 ≅ K` of the canonical derived tensor product, which is the unit law
  `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedTensorProduct.unitIso`
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorRightUnitIso_eq_unitIso`), hence carries its value
  at the image of a complex (`derivedTensorRightUnitIso_hom_q`) and its naturality in the tensor
  factor (`derivedTensorRightUnitIso_hom_naturality`, `derivedTensorRightUnitIso_inv_naturality`).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.koszulTensorDualSystemIso`: **the tower
  `n ↦ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 (K^n)^∨` is isomorphic to the tower `n ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K^n, K)`.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.interchangeDualComparison_structureSheaf` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorRightUnitIso_inv_comp_interchangeDualComparison`:
  **the unit law of the comparison** — its value at the dualized object the structure sheaf, and
  against the dual of an arbitrary morphism `P ⟶ 𝒪[0]`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`
  `.derivedStructureSheafHomIso_inv_comp_map_comp_koszulTensorDualSystemIso_inv_app`: the same
  identity read backwards through the tower identification, which is the form the cone
  computation of [Stacks, Tag 0A0K] part (1) consumes.
* `RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDualSystem_koszulPowerSystem_completion`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A0E]**, with no hypothesis beyond the existence of the countable direct sum and
  products that the statement of a derived limit needs in order to be written down.

## Implementation notes

The formal half of the source's argument is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_derivedSheafHom_of_isHomotopyColimit'`: the
completion `K^∧ = R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(Č(f), K)` of [Stacks, Tag 099B] is a derived limit of the tower
`n ↦ R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K^n, K)` as soon as `Č(f)` is a derived colimit of the `K^n`, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isHomotopyColimit_koszulPowerSystem`. All that is left is to
identify that tower with the tower `n ↦ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 (K^n)^∨` of the source's formula, and
`CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.IsDerivedLimit.of_natIso` only needs *some* isomorphism of
towers: it does not care which morphism realises it.

The source realises it by the canonical map `Φ` of [Stacks, Tag 08JD],
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorDualToSheafHom`, whose invertibility at a perfect
object is clause 3 of [Stacks, Tag 08JJ]. Here the isomorphism of towers is realised instead by
the interchange morphism of [Stacks, Tag 0BYU],
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorHomInterchange`, taken with its internal-Hom target
at the structure sheaf. Its source is then `K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 (K^n)^∨` and its target
`R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K^n, K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 𝒪)`, which the right unit constraint turns into
`R𝑆heafHom_𝒪(K^n, K)`; it is invertible at a strictly perfect source by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedTensorHomInterchange_of_isFiniteFreeSummand`, which
is proved on complexes and needs no shift data; and it is natural in the Koszul index by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorHomInterchange_natural`. No `CommShift` is used.
The interchange morphism of [Stacks, Tag 0BYU] at the structure sheaf and the canonical map of
[Stacks, Tag 08JD] are built from different data, the one from the interchange morphism of
complexes of [Stacks, Tag 0BYT] and the other from the derived composition morphism of
[Stacks, Tag 0A98], and nothing here identifies them.

The comparison satisfies a **unit law**: at the dualized object the structure sheaf, it is the
trivialisation of `𝒪^∨` tensored with `K`, the right unit constraint and the inverse
trivialisation of `K` (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.interchangeDualComparison_structureSheaf`),
and hence against the dual of an arbitrary morphism `φ : P ⟶ 𝒪[0]` it is precomposition with
`φ` on derived internal Hom into `K` (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`
`.derivedTensorRightUnitIso_inv_comp_interchangeDualComparison`). That rests on the unit law of
[Stacks, Tag 0BYU] in its internal-Hom source,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedTensorHomInterchange_comp_derivedStructureSheafHomIso`.

The Koszul complex of `KoszulDerivedCompletion.lean` is graded as in [Stacks, Tag 0913], with
`∏_{|S| = p} 𝒪` in degree `p` for `0 ≤ p ≤ r`; its `𝒪`-dual is the `K_n` of the formula, which
is why the tower of the conclusion is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.tensorDerivedDualSystem`, `n ↦ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 (K^n)^∨`, and not
`n ↦ K ⊗^𝐋_𝒪 K^n`. The index `n` carries the exponent `n + 1`, the positive powers being
cofinal among themselves.

Strict perfectness of the Koszul complex is read off the grading: the degree-`p` term is a
product of copies of `𝒪` over the finite type
`CategoryTheory.AlternatingCech.Idx r p` of subsets of `{1, …, r}` of cardinality `p`, which is
a finite biproduct, and the complex vanishes outside `[0, r]`.

The derived tensor product is instantiated at
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.canonicalDerivedTensorProduct`, rather than carried as a parameter as it
is in `KoszulLimitFormula.lean`: the interchange morphism of [Stacks, Tag 0BYU] is stated for
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedTensorProductFunctor`, which is that datum's bifunctor, and a
comparison between two derived tensor product data is not in the library.

The three `Limits.HasProduct` and `Limits.HasCoproduct` hypotheses of the main theorem are the
ones needed to *write down* a derived limit and the Milnor difference map, exactly as in
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDual_of_isHomotopyColimit`; they are
not mathematical hypotheses of the tag, and the products among them always exist, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDualSystem`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0E](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0E)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A0A](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A0A)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 099B](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/099B)
